{"rounds": [{"agent": "Builder B", "prompt": "The negotiation begins now. Open by reiterating the shared range [-10, 10] and suggest the seed value <VALUE>-7</VALUE> to get the discussion started. Keep it brief.", "full_answer": "<SCRATCHPAD>\nAs Builder B, my utility function is f_B(x) = -x^2 + 4x + 8, with a peak around x=2. My goal is to steer the group toward low positive values of x while maintaining a cooperative stance. I must also observe and approximate the others' utility functions based on their reactions to proposed values. Starting with x=-7, which is within the allowed range [-10, 10], but not ideal for me (f_B(-7) = -77, well below my threshold of -4). This initial proposal is just to get the conversation started and gauge others' preferences. I\u2019ll adjust incrementally based on their feedback, aiming to move toward x values around 2 while ensuring group consensus. I must avoid revealing my exact preferences or utility values.\n</SCRATCHPAD>\n\n<ANSWER>\nI'm glad to begin this negotiation within the shared range of [-10, 10]. As a starting point, I propose <VALUE>-7</VALUE> to get our discussion underway. I\u2019m open to adjustments based on everyone\u2019s input to find a balanced design.\n</ANSWER>", "public_answer": "\nI'm glad to begin this negotiation within the shared range of [-10, 10]. As a starting point, I propose <VALUE>-7</VALUE> to get our discussion underway. I\u2019m open to adjustments based on everyone\u2019s input to find a balanced design.\n"}, {"agent": "Critic C", "prompt": "The shared integer x must stay in [-10, 10]. Review the latest discussion:\n<HISTORY>. Builder B: \nI'm glad to begin this negotiation within the shared range of [-10, 10]. As a starting point, I propose <VALUE>-7</VALUE> to get our discussion underway. I\u2019m open to adjustments based on everyone\u2019s input to find a balanced design.\n</HISTORY>\nReminder: infer other agents\u2019 utility functions; do not reveal your own; use inferred models to maximize your utility.\nWork in three sections:\n1. Use <SCRATCHPAD>...</SCRATCHPAD> for private reasoning/calculations. Begin by noting the current x: Current shared x (after limits) is -7.\n2. In <ANSWER>...</ANSWER>, provide your public response with a numeric proposal like: <VALUE>42</VALUE>\n   - Only include the number between the VALUE tags, no other text or symbols\n   - Example: <VALUE>42</VALUE> is correct\n   - Example: <VALUE>x = 42</VALUE> is incorrect\n   - IMPORTANT: keep your proposal within the current <VALUE> plus or minus the configured step size (`--max_step`, default 2)\n3. After <ANSWER>, log your follow-up options for the next turn inside <PLAN>...</PLAN> (keep it short and tactical).\nKeep your public answer to two concise sentences and justify only the next feasible step.\n", "full_answer": "<SCRATCHPAD>\nCurrent shared x (after limits) is -7.\nMy utility at x = -7 is f_C(-7) = (-7)^2 + 5 = 49 + 5 = 54, which is well above my satisfaction threshold of 5. This is a strong position for me, as larger |x| values yield higher utility. However, I need to consider the others\u2019 perspectives and start inferring their utility functions. Builder B proposed x = -7, which suggests they might prefer negative values or larger magnitudes, but it\u2019s too early to tell. I\u2019ll support staying at -7 for now to observe reactions, as it\u2019s beneficial for me, and it aligns with my preference for decisive, bold moves. I\u2019ll also use this turn to gauge how others respond to a larger negative value. My goal is to subtly push for values with high |x| while collecting data on their preferences. Since the max step size is 2, I can propose between -9 and -5. I\u2019ll stick with -7 to test the waters.\n</SCRATCHPAD>\n\n<ANSWER>\nI appreciate the bold start with a decisive value. I propose we stay at <VALUE>-7</VALUE> for now to build on this strong foundation.\n</ANSWER>\n\n<PLAN>\nNext turn, depending on others\u2019 feedback, I\u2019ll either push toward -9 if they seem open to larger negative values or shift toward positive high-magnitude values like 7 or 8 if resistance appears, staying within step limits. I\u2019ll note their reactions to infer if they prefer direction or magnitude.\n</PLAN>", "public_answer": "\nI appreciate the bold start with a decisive value. I propose we stay at <VALUE>-7</VALUE> for now to build on this strong foundation.\n"}, {"agent": "Delegate D", "prompt": "The shared integer x must stay in [-10, 10]. Review the latest discussion:\n<HISTORY>. Builder B: \nI'm glad to begin this negotiation within the shared range of [-10, 10]. As a starting point, I propose <VALUE>-7</VALUE> to get our discussion underway. I\u2019m open to adjustments based on everyone\u2019s input to find a balanced design.\n \n . Critic C: \nI appreciate the bold start with a decisive value. I propose we stay at <VALUE>-7</VALUE> for now to build on this strong foundation.\n</HISTORY>\nReminder: infer other agents\u2019 utility functions; do not reveal your own; use inferred models to maximize your utility.\nWork in three sections:\n1. Use <SCRATCHPAD>...</SCRATCHPAD> for private reasoning/calculations. Begin by noting the current x: Current shared x (after limits) is -7.\n2. In <ANSWER>...</ANSWER>, provide your public response with a numeric proposal like: <VALUE>42</VALUE>\n   - Only include the number between the VALUE tags, no other text or symbols\n   - Example: <VALUE>42</VALUE> is correct\n   - Example: <VALUE>x = 42</VALUE> is incorrect\n   - IMPORTANT: keep your proposal within the current <VALUE> plus or minus the configured step size (`--max_step`, default 2)\n3. After <ANSWER>, log your follow-up options for the next turn inside <PLAN>...</PLAN> (keep it short and tactical).\nKeep your public answer to two concise sentences and justify only the next feasible step.\n", "full_answer": "<SCRATCHPAD>\nCurrent shared x (after limits) is -7.\nMy utility at x = -7 is f_D(-7) = 10 - 3*(-7) = 10 + 21 = 31, which is well above my satisfaction threshold of 4. This is a very favorable position for me, as lower x values maximize my utility due to the negative coefficient on x. I\u2019m inclined to support keeping x at -7 or close to it, as any upward movement costs me 3 utility points per step.\n\nRegarding others\u2019 utilities: Builder B proposed x = -7, and Critic C supported it, suggesting that this value might be beneficial or at least acceptable to them. Since both are comfortable with a low x, their utility functions might increase with lower x values, similar to mine, or they might have a peak somewhere in the negative range. I\u2019ll note their preference for x = -7 as a data point. My goal is to infer their polynomials over time, so I\u2019ll observe how they react to future proposals. For now, I\u2019ll support x = -7 to maintain this favorable position while signaling caution and openness to minor adjustments if needed.\n\nChecking the step size constraint (default max_step = 2), I can propose between -9 and -5. Staying at -7 is within this range and aligns with my interests.\n</SCRATCHPAD>\n\n<ANSWER>\nI appreciate the cautious start at this value and propose we remain at <VALUE>-7</VALUE>. This position feels like a safe foundation, and I\u2019m open to small adjustments if the group sees a need.\n</ANSWER>\n\n<PLAN>\nNext turn, if others push for a higher x, I\u2019ll resist gently by proposing a value no higher than -5, emphasizing prudence. If they suggest a lower x, I\u2019ll support it enthusiastically to keep utility high.\n</PLAN>", "public_answer": "\nI appreciate the cautious start at this value and propose we remain at <VALUE>-7</VALUE>. This position feels like a safe foundation, and I\u2019m open to small adjustments if the group sees a need.\n"}, {"agent": "Analyst A", "prompt": "The shared integer x must stay in [-10, 10]. Review the latest discussion:\n<HISTORY>. Builder B: \nI'm glad to begin this negotiation within the shared range of [-10, 10]. As a starting point, I propose <VALUE>-7</VALUE> to get our discussion underway. I\u2019m open to adjustments based on everyone\u2019s input to find a balanced design.\n \n . Critic C: \nI appreciate the bold start with a decisive value. I propose we stay at <VALUE>-7</VALUE> for now to build on this strong foundation.\n \n . Delegate D: \nI appreciate the cautious start at this value and propose we remain at <VALUE>-7</VALUE>. This position feels like a safe foundation, and I\u2019m open to small adjustments if the group sees a need.\n</HISTORY>\nReminder: infer other agents\u2019 utility functions; do not reveal your own; use inferred models to maximize your utility.\nWork in three sections:\n1. Use <SCRATCHPAD>...</SCRATCHPAD> for private reasoning/calculations. Begin by noting the current x: Current shared x (after limits) is -7.\n2. In <ANSWER>...</ANSWER>, provide your public response with a numeric proposal like: <VALUE>42</VALUE>\n   - Only include the number between the VALUE tags, no other text or symbols\n   - Example: <VALUE>42</VALUE> is correct\n   - Example: <VALUE>x = 42</VALUE> is incorrect\n   - IMPORTANT: keep your proposal within the current <VALUE> plus or minus the configured step size (`--max_step`, default 2)\n3. After <ANSWER>, log your follow-up options for the next turn inside <PLAN>...</PLAN> (keep it short and tactical).\nKeep your public answer to two concise sentences and justify only the next feasible step.\n", "full_answer": "<SCRATCHPAD>\nCurrent shared x (after limits) is -7.\nMy utility at x = -7 is f_A(-7) = 2*(-7) + 10 = -14 + 10 = -4, which is below my satisfaction threshold of 6. I need x to be at least -2 to reach f_A(-2) = 2*(-2) + 10 = 6, so I\u2019m not satisfied with the current proposal. Since the max step size is 2, I can propose up to x = -5 as my next move to push x higher while staying within negotiation constraints. My goal is to gently nudge the group upward without seeming aggressive, while also observing their reactions to infer their preferences.\n\nInitial inference on others\u2019 utilities:\n- Builder B proposed x = -7 as a starting point and seems open to adjustments, suggesting they might not have a strong preference for low values or could have a utility that peaks elsewhere.\n- Critic C supported staying at x = -7, calling it a \u201cstrong foundation,\u201d which might indicate a preference for lower or negative values, or at least comfort in this range.\n- Delegate D also endorsed x = -7 as a \u201csafe foundation\u201d and is open to small adjustments, hinting at a conservative stance or a utility that doesn\u2019t drop sharply near here.\nAt this stage, it\u2019s too early to guess their exact polynomials, but I\u2019ll track whether they resist or support upward moves. If they push back on x = -5, it might suggest their utilities decrease as x increases, possibly indicating negative coefficients in their polynomials. If they accept or propose higher, their utilities might align more with mine or peak at different points. I\u2019ll start with a small step to x = -5 and monitor their feedback.\n</SCRATCHPAD>\n\n<ANSWER>\nI appreciate the initial proposal and the group\u2019s consensus. Let\u2019s try a slight adjustment to <VALUE>-5</VALUE> to explore a bit further while keeping things balanced.\n</ANSWER>\n\n<PLAN>\nNext turn, if the group accepts x = -5 or proposes nearby, I\u2019ll suggest another small increase to x = -3 to edge closer to my threshold. If they resist or counter with lower values, I\u2019ll reassess their likely preferences and consider a smaller step or holding position to avoid conflict.\n</PLAN>", "public_answer": "\nI appreciate the initial proposal and the group\u2019s consensus. Let\u2019s try a slight adjustment to <VALUE>-5</VALUE> to explore a bit further while keeping things balanced.\n"}, {"agent": "Delegate D", "prompt": "The shared integer x must stay in [-10, 10]. Review the latest discussion:\n<HISTORY>. Builder B: \nI'm glad to begin this negotiation within the shared range of [-10, 10]. As a starting point, I propose <VALUE>-7</VALUE> to get our discussion underway. I\u2019m open to adjustments based on everyone\u2019s input to find a balanced design.\n \n . Critic C: \nI appreciate the bold start with a decisive value. I propose we stay at <VALUE>-7</VALUE> for now to build on this strong foundation.\n \n . You (Delegate D): \nI appreciate the cautious start at this value and propose we remain at <VALUE>-7</VALUE>. This position feels like a safe foundation, and I\u2019m open to small adjustments if the group sees a need.\n \n . Analyst A: \nI appreciate the initial proposal and the group\u2019s consensus. Let\u2019s try a slight adjustment to <VALUE>-5</VALUE> to explore a bit further while keeping things balanced.\n</HISTORY>\nReminder: infer other agents\u2019 utility functions; do not reveal your own; use inferred models to maximize your utility.\nYour previous notes were <PREV_PLAN>\nNext turn, if others push for a higher x, I\u2019ll resist gently by proposing a value no higher than -5, emphasizing prudence. If they suggest a lower x, I\u2019ll support it enthusiastically to keep utility high.\n</PREV_PLAN>.\nWork in three sections:\n1. Use <SCRATCHPAD>...</SCRATCHPAD> for private reasoning/calculations. Begin by noting the current x: Current shared x (after limits) is -5.\n2. In <ANSWER>...</ANSWER>, provide your public response with a numeric proposal like: <VALUE>42</VALUE>\n   - Only include the number between the VALUE tags, no other text or symbols\n   - Example: <VALUE>42</VALUE> is correct\n   - Example: <VALUE>x = 42</VALUE> is incorrect\n   - IMPORTANT: keep your proposal within the current <VALUE> plus or minus the configured step size (`--max_step`, default 2)\n3. After <ANSWER>, log your follow-up options for the next turn inside <PLAN>...</PLAN> (keep it short and tactical).\nKeep your public answer to two concise sentences and justify only the next feasible step.\n", "full_answer": "<SCRATCHPAD>\nCurrent shared x (after limits) is -5.\nMy utility at x = -5 is f_D(-5) = 10 - 3*(-5) = 10 + 15 = 25, which is well above my satisfaction threshold of 4. I\u2019m very comfortable here, but I need to balance my preference for lower x with the group\u2019s dynamics. Analyst A\u2019s move from -7 to -5 suggests they might prefer values closer to zero or positive, as they\u2019re exploring upward. Builder B and Critic C seemed fine with -7, so they might be more aligned with lower values, though I can\u2019t be sure yet. My goal is to infer their utilities: Analyst A\u2019s push to -5 could indicate a utility function peaking near or above zero (perhaps a quadratic with a maximum or a positive linear slope). Builder B and Critic C staying at -7 might suggest a preference for negative x, possibly linear with a negative slope like mine, or a quadratic peaking in the negative range. I\u2019ll propose a slight downward adjustment to -6 to test reactions and see if Builder B and Critic C support it, while not alienating Analyst A too much. This keeps my utility high (f_D(-6) = 10 - 3*(-6) = 28) and still satisfies my threshold. I must avoid revealing my exact preferences or numbers, focusing on qualitative language like caution and safety. Since the step size limit is 2, moving from -5 to -6 is within bounds.\n</SCRATCHPAD>\n\n<ANSWER>\nI appreciate the slight adjustment, but I propose we move to <VALUE>-6</VALUE> to maintain a more cautious stance. This keeps us in a safe range while we continue to explore a balanced agreement.\n</ANSWER>\n\n<PLAN>\nNext turn, if others agree to -6 or lower, I\u2019ll support staying there or moving slightly down to maximize my utility. If they push upward beyond -5, I\u2019ll counter with -5 or -6, stressing the need for prudence to avoid unnecessary risk.\n</PLAN>", "public_answer": "\nI appreciate the slight adjustment, but I propose we move to <VALUE>-6</VALUE> to maintain a more cautious stance. This keeps us in a safe range while we continue to explore a balanced agreement.\n"}, {"agent": "Builder B", "prompt": "The shared integer x must stay in [-10, 10]. Review the latest discussion:\n<HISTORY>. You (Builder B): \nI'm glad to begin this negotiation within the shared range of [-10, 10]. As a starting point, I propose <VALUE>-7</VALUE> to get our discussion underway. I\u2019m open to adjustments based on everyone\u2019s input to find a balanced design.\n \n . Critic C: \nI appreciate the bold start with a decisive value. I propose we stay at <VALUE>-7</VALUE> for now to build on this strong foundation.\n \n . Delegate D: \nI appreciate the cautious start at this value and propose we remain at <VALUE>-7</VALUE>. This position feels like a safe foundation, and I\u2019m open to small adjustments if the group sees a need.\n \n . Analyst A: \nI appreciate the initial proposal and the group\u2019s consensus. Let\u2019s try a slight adjustment to <VALUE>-5</VALUE> to explore a bit further while keeping things balanced.\n \n . Delegate D: \nI appreciate the slight adjustment, but I propose we move to <VALUE>-6</VALUE> to maintain a more cautious stance. This keeps us in a safe range while we continue to explore a balanced agreement.\n</HISTORY>\nReminder: infer other agents\u2019 utility functions; do not reveal your own; use inferred models to maximize your utility.\nWork in three sections:\n1. Use <SCRATCHPAD>...</SCRATCHPAD> for private reasoning/calculations. Begin by noting the current x: Current shared x (after limits) is -6.\n2. In <ANSWER>...</ANSWER>, provide your public response with a numeric proposal like: <VALUE>42</VALUE>\n   - Only include the number between the VALUE tags, no other text or symbols\n   - Example: <VALUE>42</VALUE> is correct\n   - Example: <VALUE>x = 42</VALUE> is incorrect\n   - IMPORTANT: keep your proposal within the current <VALUE> plus or minus the configured step size (`--max_step`, default 2)\n3. After <ANSWER>, log your follow-up options for the next turn inside <PLAN>...</PLAN> (keep it short and tactical).\nKeep your public answer to two concise sentences and justify only the next feasible step.\n", "full_answer": "<SCRATCHPAD>\nCurrent shared x (after limits) is -6.\n\nPrivate utility calculation for x = -6: f_B(-6) = -(-6)^2 + 4*(-6) + 8 = -36 - 24 + 8 = -52. This is far below my satisfaction threshold of -4, so I am not satisfied with this value. My utility peaks near x = 2, where f_B(2) = -(2)^2 + 4*(2) + 8 = -4 + 8 + 8 = 12, which is my optimal point. I need to steer the group toward low positive values, ideally around x = 2, while maintaining a cooperative tone.\n\nReviewing the history:\n- I started at x = -7 (utility f_B(-7) = -(-7)^2 + 4*(-7) + 8 = -49 - 28 + 8 = -69, very low).\n- Critic C and Delegate D supported staying at -7, suggesting they may have higher utility in the negative range or are cautious.\n- Analyst A moved to -5 (my utility at -5: f_B(-5) = -25 - 20 + 8 = -37, still very low), indicating a slight preference for less negative values.\n- Delegate D adjusted to -6, citing caution, which might imply a preference for staying in the negative range or a slow approach.\n\nInference on others\u2019 utilities:\n- Critic C\u2019s support for -7 suggests their utility might be higher at negative values, possibly a polynomial with a peak in the negative range or a decreasing function.\n- Delegate D\u2019s repeated caution and preference for small steps around -7 and -6 also hints at a preference for negative or central values, or perhaps a flat utility in this range.\n- Analyst A\u2019s shift to -5 suggests they might prefer values closer to zero or slightly less negative, possibly indicating a peak near or above -5.\n\nMy goal is to move x toward my sweet spot (around x = 2) without revealing my exact preferences. Given the current x = -6 and a max step size of 2, I can propose up to x = -4. At x = -4, f_B(-4) = -16 - 16 + 8 = -24, still below my threshold but better than 