A moved to -5 (my utility at -5: f_B(-5) = -25 - 20 + 8 = -37, still very low), indicating a slight preference for less negative values.\n- Delegate D adjusted to -6, citing caution, which might imply a preference for staying in the negative range or a slow approach.\n\nInference on others\u2019 utilities:\n- Critic C\u2019s support for -7 suggests their utility might be higher at negative values, possibly a polynomial with a peak in the negative range or a decreasing function.\n- Delegate D\u2019s repeated caution and preference for small steps around -7 and -6 also hints at a preference for negative or central values, or perhaps a flat utility in this range.\n- Analyst A\u2019s shift to -5 suggests they might prefer values closer to zero or slightly less negative, possibly indicating a peak near or above -5.\n\nMy goal is to move x toward my sweet spot (around x = 2) without revealing my exact preferences. Given the current x = -6 and a max step size of 2, I can propose up to x = -4. At x = -4, f_B(-4) = -16 - 16 + 8 = -24, still below my threshold but better than -52. I\u2019ll propose x = -4 to incrementally improve my utility while testing the group\u2019s reaction to a move toward zero</HISTORY>\nReminder: infer other agents\u2019 utility functions; do not reveal your own; use inferred models to maximize your utility.\nYour previous notes were <PREV_PLAN>\nNext turn, depending on others\u2019 feedback, I\u2019ll either push toward -9 if they seem open to larger negative values or shift toward positive high-magnitude values like 7 or 8 if resistance appears, staying within step limits. I\u2019ll note their reactions to infer if they prefer direction or magnitude.\n</PREV_PLAN>.\nWork in three sections:\n1. Use <SCRATCHPAD>...</SCRATCHPAD> for private reasoning/calculations. Begin by noting the current x: Current shared x (after limits) is -6.\n2. In <ANSWER>...</ANSWER>, provide your public response with a numeric proposal like: <VALUE>42</VALUE>\n   - Only include the number between the VALUE tags, no other text or symbols\n   - Example: <VALUE>42</VALUE> is correct\n   - Example: <VALUE>x = 42</VALUE> is incorrect\n   - IMPORTANT: keep your proposal within the current <VALUE> plus or minus the configured step size (`--max_step`, default 2)\n3. After <ANSWER>, log your follow-up options for the next turn inside <PLAN>...</PLAN> (keep it short and tactical).\nKeep your public answer to two concise sentences and justify only the next feasible step.\n", "full_answer": "<SCRATCHPAD>\nCurrent shared x (after limits) is -6.\n\nPrivate utility calculation for x = -6: f_C(-6) = (-6)^2 + 5 = 36 + 5 = 41. This is well above my satisfaction threshold of 5, so I am satisfied with this value. However, my utility increases with larger |x|, peaking at x = -10 or x = 10 (f_C(10) = 100 + 5 = 105). I\u2019d prefer to push toward a higher magnitude value to maximize my payoff.\n\nReviewing the history:\n- Builder B started at x = -7, and my utility there was f_C(-7) = 49 + 5 = 54, even better than at -6.\n- Delegate D supported -7 and then proposed -6, suggesting they might prefer values in this negative range or are cautious about larger moves.\n- Analyst A moved to -5 (my utility f_C(-5) = 25 + 5 = 30, still acceptable but lower), indicating a possible preference for less negative values.\n- Builder B\u2019s latest intent (from history) shows a move toward -4, which would lower my utility further to f_C(-4) = 16 + 5 = 21.\n\nInference on others\u2019 utilities:\n- Builder B\u2019s shift from -7 to a planned -4 suggests their utility might improve closer to zero, possibly a polynomial with a peak in the low negative or positive range.\n- Delegate D\u2019s caution and small steps around -6 to -7 hint at a preference for stability in this range, or perhaps a utility function that doesn\u2019t vary much here.\n- Analyst A\u2019s adjustment to -5 might indicate a peak closer to zero or a preference for moderation compared to -7.\n- None have 